<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Gambia</h1><p class="st-meta">1965 CE – present · capital Banjul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The smallest country on the African mainland, independent from Britain in 1965, which survived a 1994 coup and a 2016 crisis when Yahya Jammeh refused to go.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gambia-colony-and-protectorate">Gambia Colony and Protectorate</a> achieved independence on 18 February 1965 as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> within the Commonwealth, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> as Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">The Gambia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawda-jawara">Dawda Jawara</a>, who had held office since 1962, led the country, and it became a republic on 24 April 1970 after a second referendum.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Jawara was re-elected five times as president. An attempted coup on 29 July 1981 was defeated with Senegalese troops, and between 500 and 800 people were killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> formed the Senegambia Confederation in 1982, from which the Gambia withdrew in 1989. In 1994 the Armed Forces Provisional Ruling Council deposed Jawara, and Lieutenant Yahya Jammeh became head of state. He was elected president in 1996 and 1997 elections and ruled for 22 years. In 2013 his government took the country out of the Commonwealth.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Adama Barrow won the election of 1 December 2016. Jammeh first accepted the result, then declared it void, and an ECOWAS intervention led to his departure on 20 January 2017. Barrow removed the word Islamic from the country's name and brought it back into the Commonwealth on 8 February 2018. He was re-elected on 4 December 2021.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gambia.jpg" alt="Gambia" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Vzb83 i The source code of the previous SVG was invalid due to 39 errors. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_The_Gambia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1965 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Banjul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dawda Jawara, Yahya Jammeh, Adama Barrow</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam (mostly Sunni), Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/gambia">Gambia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>The Gambia, Republic of The Gambia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1996&amp;focus=gambia" aria-label="Gambia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4226 1677 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gambia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4546 1933L4546 1933"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4546 1933h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4546 1933h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4546 1933h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Banjul. <a href="/atlas?year=1996&amp;focus=gambia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
